Q: Sending raw JSON using Postman value is null A: <p>When you don’t specify where the `String name` argument comes from, Spring looks for it as a **Query Parameter** (in the URL like `?name=wolverine`) or as standard **Form Data** fields. It completely ignores the HTTP request body where your raw JSON lives, you need to tell Spring to look inside the JSON body, and you also need to make sure your Java backend can map that JSON structure properly.</p>
<p>Fix your Java Controller using a DTO</p>
<pre><code>```java
public class HeroRequest {
    private String name;

    // Getters and Setters
    public String getName() { return name; }
    public void setName(String name) { this.name = name; }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Add the <code>@RequestBody</code> annotation to tell Spring to read the raw HTTP request body and deserialize the JSON into your new object.</p>
<pre><code>@RestController
public class HeroController {

    @PostMapping(&quot;/displayHero&quot;) // Shorthand for RequestMethod.POST
    public Map&lt;String, String&gt; displayInfo(@RequestBody HeroRequest request) {
        Map&lt;String, String&gt; imap = new LinkedHashMap&lt;&gt;();

        // Access the name via the DTO getter
        imap.put(&quot;hero&quot;, request.getName()); 
        return imap;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Since you changed the backend to expect an object with a <code>name</code> field, make sure your raw JSON body in Postman looks like this:</p>
<pre><code>{
    &quot;name&quot;: &quot;wolverine&quot;
}
</code></pre>

Q: Resizing N # of squares to be as big as possible while still fitting into box of X by Y dimensions. (Thumbnails!) A: <p>There is an O(1) algorithm which solves this problem correctly and can be extended to handle rectangles too. A TypeScript version which returns the side length of the square and the number of rows and columns the grid has:</p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>type Dimensions = {
    rectWidth: number,
    rectHeight: number,
    rows: number,
    columns: number
}

function gridDimensions(
  numSquares: number,
  width: number,
  height: number,
  rectRatio: number
): Dimensions {
  const ratio = (width / rectRatio) / height
  if (ratio &lt; 1) {
    const d = gridDimensions(numSquares, height, width, 1 / rectRatio)
    return {
      rectWidth: d.rectHeight,
      rectHeight: d.rectWidth,
      rows: d.columns,
      columns: d.rows
    }
  }

  const b0 = Math.ceil(Math.sqrt(numSquares / ratio))
  let res: Dimensions = { rectWidth: 0, rectHeight: 0, rows: 0, columns: 0 }

  for (let b = b0 - 1; b &lt;= b0 + 1; b++) {
    if (b == 0) {
      continue
    }

    const a = Math.ceil(numSquares / b)
    const s = Math.min((width / rectRatio) / a, height / b)

    if (s &gt; res.rectHeight) {
      res = { rectWidth: s * rectRatio, rectHeight: s, rows: b, columns: a }
    }
  }

  return res
}
</code></pre>
<p>A short version which works only with squares and returns just the side length is, which better highlights the structure of the solution:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function maximalSquareSideLength(n, w, h) {
  const r = w / r
  if (r &lt; 1) return maximalSquareSideLength(n, h, w)
  const s = (b) =&gt; Math.min(w / Math.ceil(n / b), h / b)
  const b0 = Math.ceil(Math.sqrt(n / r))
  return Math.max(s(b0 - 1), s(b0), s(b0 + 1))
}
</code></pre>
<p>The code above elides the <code>b == 0</code> check since <code>s(0) = Math.min(w / Inf, Inf) = Math.min(0, Inf) = 0</code>, becoming effectively a no-op. Re-add the condition back if in your environment division by zero raises an exception.</p>
<p>This solution exploits two particularities of the problem:</p>
<ol>
<li>It is symmetric with respect to the ratio of box dimensions: solving for a wide box with ratio <code>r</code> gives the same solution as for a tall box with ratio <code>1/r</code> but with flipped dimensions – rows become columns, columns become rows; it's just a rotation by 90 degrees</li>
<li>It's easy to see that the ideal number of rows must be around <code>sqrt(n * h/w)</code>; it can be further proven that for <code>r = w/h &gt;= 1</code> the optimal solution will always be one of <code>{ b0 - 1, b0, b0 + 1 }</code> with <code>b0 = ceil(sqrt(n * h/w))</code>.</li>
</ol>
<p><a href="https://archive.quateo.com/grid/article.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Check out this article I've written</a> for a complete mathematical proof of the above, along with a step-by-step deduction of multiple algorithms posted in this thread. <a href="https://math.stackexchange.com/a/5138148/1761953">My Mathematics Stack Exchange answer</a> has another, slightly more rigorous proof.</p>
<p>The other O(1) solution shared here gives wrong answers in many cases (for example, <code>n = 8, r = 5</code>). It can be shown it is always wrong for <code>r &gt;= 1</code> when the solution which fills the width of the box fully is strictly better than any solution which fills the height and the number of columns is strictly greater than <code>ceil(sqrt(n * w/h))</code>. For this proof check out <a href="https://archive.quateo.com/grid/long.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">my other article</a>, which shows how that algorithm can be deduced and then explains when it fails.</p>

Q: How to read a semicolon-separated CSV file into a List of custom objects in C# using File.ReadAllLines? A: <p>You can achieve this by using <code>File.ReadAllLines()</code> from the <code>System.IO</code> namespace to read the file into a string array, and then parsing each line inside a traditional <code>for</code> loop.</p>
<p>Here is the complete implementation of the custom class with an overridden <code>ToString()</code> method and the file reading logic:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public class MyButton
{
    private string text;
    private int width;
    private int height;
    private int left;
    private int top;
    private string myEvent;

    public string Text { get =&gt; text; set =&gt; text = value; }
    public int Width { get =&gt; width; set =&gt; width = value; }
    public int Height { get =&gt; height; set =&gt; height = value; }
    public int Left { get =&gt; left; set =&gt; left = value; }
    public int Top { get =&gt; top; set =&gt; top = value; }
    public string MyEvent { get =&gt; myEvent; set =&gt; myEvent = value; }

    public MyButton(string text, int width, int height, int left, int top, string myEvent)
    {
        this.Text = text;
        this.Width = width;
        this.Height = height;
        this.Left = left;
        this.Top = top;
        this.MyEvent = myEvent;
    }

    public override string ToString()
    {
        return $&quot;{Text} ({MyEvent}) - {Width}x{Height} - ({Left}, {Top})&quot;;
    }
}
Make sure your file is in the bin/Debug folder.
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.IO;

class Program
{
    static List&lt;MyButton&gt; Buttons = new List&lt;MyButton&gt;();

    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        Beolvasas(&quot;buttons.csv&quot;);
        Console.WriteLine($&quot;Successful read! The list contains {Buttons.Count} buttons.&quot;);
    }

    static void Beolvasas(string fnev)
    {
        string[] lines = File.ReadAllLines(fnev);

        for (int i = 0; i &lt; lines.Length; i++)
        {
            string[] row = lines[i].Split(';');

            MyButton gomb = new MyButton(
                row[0], 
                int.Parse(row[1]), 
                int.Parse(row[2]), 
                int.Parse(row[3]), 
                int.Parse(row[4]), 
                row[5]
            );

            Buttons.Add(gomb);
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Any way to get my .ROBLOSECURITY Cookie from Browser Dev Console? A: <p>Using <code>document.cookie</code> to get the <code>.ROBLOSECURITY</code> cookie. Remember that instead of using Javascript in the console you can directly find the cookie in the inspector. I have not tested this yet on the Roblox website, but this is my code:
IMPORTANT: The <code>.ROBLOSECURITY</code> cookie is a httponly cookie, so you need to change this in the sources or application tab of the inspector.</p>
<pre><code>// Function to update the loading bar in the console
console.log(&quot;Cookie search in progress...&quot;);
var updateLoadingBarInConsole = function(progress) {
  var length = 50; // Length of the loading bar
  var filledLength = Math.round(length * progress / 100);
  var bar = '[' + '='.repeat(filledLength) + ' '.repeat(length - filledLength) + ']';
  console.log(bar + ' ' + progress + '%');
};

// Function to get all cookies and track progress in console
var getAllCookies = function() {
  var cookies = document.cookie.split(';').reduce(function(acc, cookie, index, array) {
    // Calculate progress and update the loading bar in the console

    var progress = Math.floor(((index + 1) / array.length) * 100);
    updateLoadingBarInConsole(progress);

    var parts = cookie.split('=');
    if (parts.length === 2) {
      var cookieName = parts[0].trim();
      var cookieValue = decodeURIComponent(parts[1].trim());
      acc[cookieName] = cookieValue;
      return acc;
    }
    return acc;
  }, {});

  return cookies;
};

// Get all cookies and check for the specific cookie
var allCookies = getAllCookies();
if (allCookies['.ROBLOSECURITY']) {
  console.log('.ROBLOSECURITY cookie has been found! value = ' + allCookies['.ROBLOSECURITY']);
}
</code></pre>

Q: IllegalArgumentException: width and height must be &gt; 0 A: <p>If you want to get Screen Size of Device then do it as follows</p>

<pre><code>Display display = getWindowManager().getDefaultDisplay();
Point size = new Point();
display.getSize(size);
int width = size.x;
int height = size.y;
</code></pre>

<p>and if you are using in API &lt; 13 then use</p>

<pre><code>Display display = getWindowManager().getDefaultDisplay(); 
int width = display.getWidth();  // deprecated
int height = display.getHeight();
</code></pre>

<p>And then use these values in</p>

<pre><code>_background = getResizedBitmap(background,height,width); 
</code></pre>

<p>Reference <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/4743116/get-screen-width-and-height">ScreenSize</a></p>
